  <!-- § 11 the cost of heat: inputs climate-era5-energy (cdd65), climate-cmip6-windows-energy (cdd65), climate-heat-deaths, climate-burned-area -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>What a hotter day costs</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Cooling, health and fire</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>A hotter day has three costs that can be counted, and several that these sources cannot. It raises the electricity needed to keep a room liveable. It raises the chance of dying, mostly for the old, the poor and outdoor workers. And it cuts the hours a person can work outside at full effort.</p>
        <p>We do not know, from these sources, how many working hours heat already costs India, or how much it trims a wheat or rice harvest; those are research estimates built on the measures here, and they vary widely with method. What follows is what the sources do publish.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="break" style="margin-top:40px">
      <figure id="cb-cool" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The heat a building must shed each year has risen {fmt.count(100 * (C.cost.cddRecent / C.cost.cddBase - 1))}% since {w(W.base)}, and the models project far more</h3>
            <div class="sub">Cooling degree days at an average spot in India (each degree a day's average sits above 65°F, 18.3°C, summed over the year). Observed, ERA5, 1950 to {C.century.era5Last}, solid; projected, CMIP6 median, 20-year windows, dashed.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-cool">{fmt.inr(C.cost.cdd.at(-1)[1])}</div><div class="c">cooling degree days, {C.cost.cdd.at(-1)[0]}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-cool" set:html={CH.cool(C)} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Copernicus ERA5 and CMIP6, cooling degree days by the World Bank CCKP. Two instruments: the observed line and the projected lines are never joined.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://climateknowledgeportal.worldbank.org/country/india" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Cooling degree days measure the heat a building has to get rid of to stay at a comfortable temperature, and so track the work fans, coolers and air conditioners must do. They are the standard input to estimates of electricity demand for cooling.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {C.century.era5Last}</span>
            <p>Observed: <b id="ex-cl-base">{fmt.inr(C.cost.cddBase)}</b> a year on average in {w(W.base)}, <b id="ex-cl-recent">{fmt.inr(C.cost.cddRecent)}</b> in {w(W.recent)}. Projected from the models' 1995-2014 level of {fmt.inr(P.cdd65.ref)}: {fmt.inr(P.cdd65.s[0].w.find((x) => x.y === 2040).med)} in 2040-2059 under SSP2-4.5, {fmt.inr(P.cdd65.s[1].w.find((x) => x.y === 2080).med)} by 2080-2099 under SSP5-8.5.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-deaths" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Under middling emissions, temperature-related deaths are projected to rise by about {fmt.num1(hdLast.at(-1)[1])} per 100,000 people a year by the 2090s</h3>
            <div class="sub">Projected change in India's death rate from non-optimal temperatures, deaths per 100,000 people a year, against today, under RCP4.5 (a pathway close to SSP2-4.5).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-deaths">+{fmt.num1(hdLast.at(-1)[1])}</div><div class="c">per 100,000, {hdLast.at(-1)[0]}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-deaths" set:html={CH.deaths(C)} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Climate Impact Lab and UNDP, Human Climate Horizons (2024), via Our World in Data (CC BY 4.0). A model estimate; adaptation and income growth are assumed as in the source.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://horizons.hdr.undp.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The figure is the change in deaths from heat and cold combined: warmer winters save some lives, hotter summers cost more. It is a projection from statistical links between temperature and deaths, observed across many countries, applied to India's projected climate and population. India's death registration does not record heat as a cause reliably enough to count these deaths directly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of Human Climate Horizons, 2024</span>
            <p>Projected change: <b id="ex-d-30">+{fmt.num1(hdLast[0][1])}</b> per 100,000 a year by {hdLast[0][0]}, +{fmt.num1(hdLast[1][1])} by {hdLast[1][0]} and +{fmt.num1(hdLast[2][1])} by {hdLast[2][0]}. On India's population of {E.year}, +{fmt.num1(hdLast[1][1])} per 100,000 would be about {lakh(hdLast[1][1] * S.IND.pop / 1e5)} more deaths a year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-fires" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Most of the land that burns in India each year is farmland</h3>
            <div class="sub">Area burnt by land type, lakh hectares a year, 2002 to {fires.firesAll.at(-1)[0]}, from satellite detection.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-fires">{fmt.num1(cropShare)}%</div><div class="c">croplands, {fires.firesAll.at(-1)[0]}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-fires" set:html={CH.fires(C)} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Global Wildfire Information System (GWIS), via Our World in Data (CC BY 4.0). Burnt area seen by satellite; small fires can be missed.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://gwis.jrc.ec.europa.eu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A satellite sees a scorch mark, not its cause. In India, most burnt cropland is stubble set alight after the harvest, in Punjab and Haryana after rice in October and November, and across the plains before the summer crop. Its smoke adds to north India's winter haze. In the weekly satellite record, burning peaks in March and April and again in October and November.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {fires.firesAll.at(-1)[0]}</span>
            <p>Area burnt: <b id="ex-fi-all">{lakh(fires.firesAll.at(-1)[1])} hectares</b>, of which croplands {lakh(fires.firesCrop.at(-1)[1])} and forests {lakh(fires.fires.find((f) => f.k === "forest").data.at(-1)[1])}.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

