  <!-- § 13 what this measures -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">13</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Temperature here is the air two metres above the ground, averaged over a day, a month or a year and over a country's or a state's land. Rain is what reaches the ground, in millimetres of water. The indices (hot days, warm nights, wet-bulb and heat-index days, dry spells, the wettest five days) are counted each day in each grid cell and then averaged over the area, so they describe an average place, not any one town.</p>
      <p>Emissions are estimates built from fuel use, industrial output and land statistics, not measurements of the air. Carbon dioxide from fossil fuels is the best measured; methane and nitrous oxide from farms are less certain, and emissions from land-use change the least.</p>
      <p>The guide reads every series the sources publish for India. The OWID datasets and the World Bank portal publish many more indices, aggregations and model runs than a page can draw: everything baked is downloadable below, and the full catalogue of what the sources publish, with what this guide did with each item, is in the repository's coverage ledgers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 14 misreadings -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">14</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"This month was normal, so the warming has paused."</h3><p>The normal is 1991-2020, already about half a degree warmer than the 1950s. A month at the normal is a warm month by the standard of the last century. One month, or one year, is weather; the change shows in thirty-year averages.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"Temperatures have fallen for three months running: it is cooling down."</h3><p>India's average temperature falls every year from May into the monsoon, as cloud and rain arrive. A month's level can be compared only with the same month in other years. Watch the anomaly, the gap from that month's normal: it can rise while the thermometer falls.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"India has warmed by less than a degree, which no one can feel."</h3><p>The average hides the extremes people live through. Nights of 26°C or more and humid days above a wet-bulb of 27°C have multiplied, and those are what tire a body, cut work hours and push up power demand.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"Delhi has not warmed."</h3><p>One record finds Delhi's afternoons little changed since the 1950s; both records find its nights clearly warmer and its humid days far more frequent. Irrigation and haze cool the afternoon and add moisture; the heat moves into the night and into the humidity.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"India is the third-largest polluter, so it is one of the main causes of climate change."</h3><p>India emits the third-largest share of the world's carbon dioxide today, but warming follows everything emitted since the 1750s, and India's share of that is a fraction of the United States' or China's. Per person, India emits less than half the world average.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">6</span><div><h3>"The models say India will be 4°C hotter."</h3><p>Only under the highest-emission pathway, by the end of the century, and as the median of a wide model range. Under lower pathways the projected warming is half that or less. Projections describe consequences of choices, not a forecast.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">7</span><div><h3>"The monsoon is failing."</h3><p>The national total shows no long decline over 125 years, and its timing has shifted only slightly. What has changed is how rain is spread: some regions are drier, others wetter, and heavy downpours are a local story that national averages blur.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

