  <footer class="provenance">
    <h2 class="prov-h">Sources, method and revisions</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Sources of record</h6>
        <p>Temperature, rain and climate indices: the World Bank Climate Change Knowledge Portal (CCKP), which aggregates CRU TS 4.10 (Climatic Research Unit, University of East Anglia), Copernicus ERA5 (ECMWF) and the CMIP6 model ensemble to India and its states. Monthly temperature, precipitation, wildfires, emissions, energy and heat-death projections: Our World in Data, with Copernicus, the Global Carbon Project, Jones et al., the Energy Institute, Ember, GWIS and the Climate Impact Lab as the underlying producers, under CC BY 4.0. Data vintage <b id="prov-vintage">{V}</b>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Method</h6>
        <p>Values are as published. Changes are differences between two 30-year averages computed within one instrument; warming rates are least-squares lines through annual values. The century plate subtracts each record's own 1951-1980 average. CCKP's fill code for a missing value is left empty. Nothing is estimated, interpolated or spliced.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Revisions and breaks</h6>
        <p>Station, reanalysis and model records are separate instruments and never joined. OWID's annual figure for the current, unfinished year is not shown. CCKP's simplified wet-bulb globe temperature series are not used: for 2024 and 2025 they carry corrupt values. The projected wet-bulb series, and one CMIP6 percentile series that CCKP serves on the wrong side of its own median, are withheld as source defects.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>This guide</h6>
        <p>Version 1.0, October 2026. The monthly reading is revised as OWID publishes each ERA5 month; the long records when CCKP adds a year; emissions when the Global Carbon Project publishes its annual budget.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="fine">
      <span>India Atlas, climate and temperature</span>
      <span>Vintage <span id="prov-vintage2">{V}</span></span>
      <span>Set in Newsreader and Libre Franklin</span>
    </div>
  </footer>

</div>
<style>
  .explorer-note{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-soft);max-width:880px;margin:0 0 16px}
  #explorer th{cursor:pointer;white-space:nowrap}
  #explorer th.n,#explorer td.n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem}
  #explorer td.name{font-size:1rem}
  #explorer th[aria-sort]{color:var(--ink)}
</style>
<script>
  import "../../dossiers/guide.client.js";
  // every state: the explorer table sorts on a click of its column head
  const table = document.getElementById("explorer");
  table?.querySelectorAll("th").forEach((th, col) => th.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const tbody = table.tBodies[0], rows = [...tbody.rows];
    const asc = th.getAttribute("aria-sort") === "descending";
    table.querySelectorAll("th").forEach((h) => h.removeAttribute("aria-sort"));
    th.setAttribute("aria-sort", asc ? "ascending" : "descending");
    const val = (r) => (col === 0 ? r.cells[0].textContent : parseFloat(r.cells[col].dataset.v));
    rows.sort((a, b) => {
      const x = val(a), y = val(b);
      if (col === 0) return asc ? x.localeCompare(y) : y.localeCompare(x);
      if (Number.isNaN(x)) return 1; if (Number.isNaN(y)) return -1;
      return asc ? x - y : y - x;
    });
    rows.forEach((r) => tbody.append(r));
  }));
</script>
